<h1>Auto-Fix Engine</h1>
<p class="subtitle">A pattern-based migration helper, not a semantic refactorer. Preview it, inspect every file, and run your application tests.</p>

<h2 id="commands">Commands</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Preview source-line rewrites</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --dry-run</span>

<span class="cmt"># Default: all tracked files</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix</span>

<span class="cmt"># Narrower scopes</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --staged</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix src/app.js config/</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --file src/app.js</span>

<span class="cmt"># Restore the latest batch-fix snapshot when safe</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate fix --undo</span></div>

<p><code>gate fix</code> with no explicit target uses <code>git ls-files</code>, so it does not include untracked files. The command rescans its selected targets and exits non-zero if findings remain or a file could not be scanned.</p>

<h2 id="batch">What a batch fix changes</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Creates a local snapshot under <code>~/.gate/snapshots/&lt;repository-hash&gt;/</code>.</li>
  <li>Attempts a line-based rewrite for each finding.</li>
  <li>Writes extracted values to a repository-root <code>.env</code>.</li>
  <li>Adds an exact <code>.env</code> line to <code>.gitignore</code> when missing.</li>
  <li>Adds placeholder names to <code>.env.example</code>.</li>
  <li>Checks whether extracted values remain in changed non-JSON source files, then the CLI runs a full pattern rescan of the targets.</li>
</ol>
<p>Core source rewrites generally use a same-directory temporary file and rename. The <code>.env</code>, <code>.gitignore</code>, <code>.env.example</code>, manifest, and some specialized paths do not all share that atomic boundary.</p>

<h2 id="languages">Current rewrite forms</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Files</th><th>Generated reference</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>JS/JSX/MJS/CJS/TS/TSX</td><td><code>process.env.NAME</code></td><td>Adds dotenv setup only when <code>dotenv</code> is already declared; browser and bundled code may not have <code>process.env</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Python</td><td><code>os.environ["NAME"]</code></td><td>May insert <code>import os</code>; does not provide environment loading.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Go</td><td><code>os.Getenv("NAME")</code></td><td>Attempts to edit imports with text patterns, not a Go parser.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ruby</td><td><code>ENV["NAME"]</code></td><td>No framework-specific loading or validation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Java</td><td><code>System.getenv("NAME")</code></td><td>No configuration binding or null handling.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>YAML</td><td><code>${NAME}</code></td><td>Many YAML consumers do not interpolate this syntax.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Terraform</td><td><code>var.name</code></td><td>Does not create a variable declaration or secure Terraform input; the extracted value still goes to <code>.env</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dockerfile</td><td><code>ENV KEY=…</code> becomes <code>ARG KEY</code></td><td>Build arguments are not a secret store and may remain observable in build systems or image history.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>JSON</td><td>No source rewrite</td><td>Extracts to <code>.env</code> and marks the item fixed internally, but leaves JSON unchanged; the command's final rescan should still fail.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unknown extension</td><td>Defaults to <code>process.env.NAME</code></td><td>Can write JavaScript syntax into a non-JavaScript file. Do not auto-fix unknown types.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="text">Textual matching limits</h2>
<p>The fixer operates on the reported physical line. It replaces a quoted secret, a larger quoted value containing the match, or a bare matching substring. It does not parse an AST, understand scopes, update tests, validate types, or know how configuration reaches production.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Multiple findings on one line can interact.</li>
  <li>Inserted imports can shift later line numbers; Gate has limited adjustment logic.</li>
  <li>Concatenation, templates, generated files, framework loaders, and unusual quoting can be wrong or unfixable.</li>
  <li>YAML/Terraform/Docker substitutions may be syntactically valid but operationally unsafe.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="env">Environment-file behavior</h2>
<p>Known rule IDs map to names such as <code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code> or <code>DATABASE_URL</code>; otherwise Gate derives a variable name from a simple JavaScript declaration or the rule ID. Values containing certain special characters are double-quoted and escaped.</p>
<div class="warning-box"><strong>Existing-name conflict bug:</strong> Gate rewrites source to the canonical name before updating <code>.env</code>. If that name already exists with a different value, Gate writes <code>NAME_NEW</code> but does not update the source reference from <code>NAME</code>. Multiple different secrets mapped to the same name have the same risk. Resolve these conflicts manually before running the application.</div>
<p>Requesting mode <code>0600</code> when writing does not necessarily tighten an already-existing file's permissions. Confirm the resulting mode, ownership, backups, sync destinations, and deployment process. <code>.gitignore</code> only affects future Git selection; it does not remove a file already tracked or a secret already committed.</p>

<h2 id="dry-run">Dry run is a partial preview</h2>
<p><code>--dry-run</code> shows proposed per-line changes and extracted variable names. It does not preview every import insertion, <code>.env</code> conflict rename, <code>.gitignore</code> edit, <code>.env.example</code> edit, snapshot, or runtime consequence. It is useful, but not a transaction plan.</p>

<h2 id="snapshots">Snapshots and undo</h2>
<p>Batch fix snapshots can contain the original source and <code>.env</code> in plaintext. Gate keeps up to ten snapshot directories per repository hash and requests restrictive directory/manifest modes. Protect <code>~/.gate/snapshots</code> like any other secret-bearing store.</p>
<p><code>gate fix --undo</code> restores or removes files from the newest snapshot only when their current hashes still match the post-fix manifest. Files changed since the fix are skipped to avoid overwriting later work. The used snapshot is removed after the attempt, including when some files conflict.</p>
<p>Per-finding fixes chosen inside the interactive walkthrough call the lower-level fixer directly and do not create the same batch snapshot or <code>.env.example</code> workflow.</p>

<h2 id="vault">The interactive vault option</h2>
<p>The <code>v</code> action encrypts a value into <code>~/.gate/vault.json</code> and replaces matching source text with <code>VAULT:&lt;12-hex-id&gt;</code>. Gate has an internal retrieval function but no CLI/runtime integration that teaches your application to resolve that marker. The replacement can therefore break the application until you build the integration yourself.</p>

<h2 id="safe-use">Safer workflow</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Rotate anything exposed outside the working tree.</li>
  <li>Start from a clean, backed-up branch.</li>
  <li>Use explicit targets and <code>--dry-run</code>.</li>
  <li>Apply the change, then inspect source, imports, <code>.env</code>, <code>.gitignore</code>, and <code>.env.example</code>.</li>
  <li>Run formatter, compiler/type checker, tests, and a manual application start.</li>
  <li>Run Gate and an independent scanner again.</li>
  <li>Commit only the reviewed source and placeholder files—never the extracted secret.</li>
</ol>
